  "question": {
    "text": "If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>53</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math> metre is the radius of smallest electron orbit in hydrogen like atom, then the atom is"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "hydrogen atom",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>He</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Li</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Be</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>For hydrogen like atom, the radius of n<sup>th</sup> orbit is given by,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi></mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></p><p>Where, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>53</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math></p><p>and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>53</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>At ground state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;</p><p>Hence, the atom is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Be</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo><mo>&#8757;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>]</mo></math></p><p>So, the atom is triply ionised beryllium <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Be</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>.</p>"
  }
}